{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"2b4333f04533c137","type":"text","text":"Fast and Efficient Hardware Implementation of HQC\nhttps://eprint.iacr.org/2022/1183","x":-200,"y":-300,"width":585,"height":100},
		{"id":"20994a817c426765","type":"text","text":"Algorithm 2 (HQC.PKE.KeyGen() and HQC.KEM.KeyGen())\n- Hamming weights $w$ are hardcoded depending on security level\n1) $h \\leftarrow \\textit{R}$\n\t- A polynomial $h$ is chosen uniformly randomly from the quotient ring $\\textit{R}$ on which vectors and operations of HQC are defined\n\t- So, a random binary vector of length $n$\n2) $(x,y) \\leftarrow^w \\textit{R}^2$\n\t- Polynomials $(x,y)$ are chosen uniformly random from $R^2$ (I believe a cartesian product) with a Hamming weight of $w$ (Choose $w$ positions to be 1, rest 0)\n\t- Something about sparsity here and how it's analogous to LWE's short vectors (?)\n\t- $(x,y)$ are the secret vectors of HQC\n3) $s := x+h\\cdot y$ \n\t- Let s be $x+h\\cdot y$\n\t- Since h and y are binary vectors, product is cyclical convolution with coefficients mod 2\n\t\t- $h$ is the random matrix\n\t\t- $y$ is the secret key\n\t\t- $x$ is the error term\n4) **return** $(pk := (h,s), sk := (x,y))$\n\t- Public key is the pair, random polynomial $h$ and the computed polynomial $s$\n\t- Secret key is the pair of sparse polynomials $(x,y)$","x":-200,"y":-140,"width":585,"height":820},
		{"id":"c988c97f555a4499","type":"text","text":"Algorithm 3 HQC.PKE.Encrypt(pk = (h, s), m, $\\theta$)\n- Provided the public key (containing random polynomial $h$ and polynomial compute $s$), the message $m$, and a noise generated seed, $\\theta$\n- Hamming weights $w_r$ are hardcoded depending on security level\n\t- Not really sure where $\\theta$ comes from yet - PRG maybe?\n1) $(r_1, r_2, e) \\leftarrow^{w_r, \\theta} \\textit{R}^3$ \n\t- Masking noise polynomials $r_1, r_2$ are sampled uniformly random from $\\textit{R}^3$ with Hamming weight $w_r$\n\t- Message noise polynomial $e$ is sampled uniformly random from $R^3$ with Hamming weight $\\theta$\n2) $u := r_1 + h \\cdot r_2$\n\t- Let $u$ be $r_1 + h \\cdot r_2$\n\t- Same as $s$ compute in KeyGen algorithm\n\t- Encodes $h$ with noisy polynomials $r_1, r_2$\n3) $t := Encode(m)$\n\t- Let $t$ be the encoded message $m$\n\t- Encoding is done by matrix vector multiplication between a hardcoded Generator matrix and a message - encode function of concatenated Reed-Muller and Reed-Solon codes\n\t\t- Stated in Definition 3.4.1 in HQC specifications:\n\t\t  \n\t- The paper noted that given $c = mG$, the codeword is given by duplicating $c$ 3 or 5 times depending on security level\n4) $v := t + s \\cdot r_2 + e$\n\t- Let $v$ be the compute of the masked message (hides encoded message using public key with some noise $e$)\n5) **return** $c := (u,v)$\n\t- Returns the ciphertext comprised of compute polynomials $u,v$","x":460,"y":-140,"width":585,"height":820},
		{"id":"60c2fef383b74e2b","type":"text","text":"Algorithm 4 HQC.PKE.Decrypt(sk = (x, y), c = (u, v))\n- Provided the two generated key's secret key $x,y$ and the ciphertext $u,v$ \n1) $m' := Decode(v - u \\cdot y)$\n\t- Let $m'$ be the decoded message $m$\n\t- Decoding is done by matrix vector multiplication between a hardcoded Generator matrix and a message - encode function of concatenated Reed-Muller and Reed-Solon codes\n\t- The paper noted that given $c = mG$, the codeword is given by duplicating $c$ 3 or 5 times depending on security level\n\t- Noted in the specifications: \n\t\t- Decoder is an algebraic decoder for the Reed-Solomon code\n2) **return** $m'$","x":1120,"y":-140,"width":585,"height":820},
		{"id":"5eebb1b7627d85d0","type":"text","text":"RS Encoding;\nPer the HQC specifications\n- Encode function takes K-bit input and first encodes it with the Reed-Solomon code\n- Let $p$ be a prime number and $q$ is any power of $p$ \n\t- Block length $n=q-1$\n\t- Min distance $d_{min}=2\\delta +1$\n\t\t- $2\\delta = n-k$ with $\\delta$ as the correcting cap of the code and $k$ as the number of info bits\n\t- $\\alpha$ is a primitive element in the binary field - the generator polynomial $g(x) = (x+\\alpha)(x+\\alpha^2)...(x+\\alpha^{2\\delta})$ \n- For HQC, the binary field $\\mathbb{F}_{2^m}$ where $m=8$ \n\t- Thus primitive polynomial $g(x) = 1+\\alpha^2+\\alpha^3+\\alpha^4+\\alpha^8$\n\nThe encoding process can be summarized as such:\nEncoding is was summarized as the following systemic flow:\n1) Multiply the message u(x) by $x^{n-k}$\n\t- $x^{n-k} \\Rightarrow$ Shift message left by $n-k$ positions \n2) Compute the remainder b(x) by dividing $x^{n-k}u(x)$ by gen poly g(x)\n\t- $b(x) = x^{n-k}u(x)\\: \\text{mod}\\: g(x)$ \n\t- So, $b(x)$ is the parity shift\n3) Combine b(x) and x^{x-k}u(x) to obtain the code polynomial $c(x) = b(x) + x^{n-k}u(x)$\n\nRM Encoding:\nPer the HQC specifications\n- HQC uses duplicate RM codes, where using first order RM (denoted RM(1,7)) ","x":460,"y":1115,"width":585,"height":880},
		{"id":"98313aa33eefbe61","type":"text","text":"Deshpande et al:\n- Achieves step (1) by passing $m_{in}$ of length $K$  into a shift register\n- Achieves step (2) by passing Shifted $m_{in}$ into Galois Field multipliers\n\t- Galois multipliers were said to have been designed via an LFSR-based optimization multiplication unit \n\t- Galois multipliers are only used in Solomon encoding\n- Achieves step (3) by passing Galois mult outputs into LSFR after each cycle","x":460,"y":760,"width":585,"height":275},
		{"id":"80d9aee55855941a","type":"text","text":"Per this article: https://tomverbeure.github.io/2022/08/07/Reed-Solomon.html (Reed-Solomon encoding)\nExample;\n- Given:\n\t- Alphabets are ints\n\t- Size $k$ of message $m$ is $4$\n\t- Want $2$ redundant symbols so code word has length $n=6$ \n\t\t- Since $6-4=2$ \n\t- Polynomial $p(x)$ is always evaluated for the following values of $x: (-1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4)$ . Note there are $x_i$ values as there are symbols in the code word\n- Encoding:\n\t- Create polynomial $p(x)=2+3x-5x^2+x^3$ (Uses message word symbols as coefficients)\n\t- Evaluate $p(x)$ at the 6 locations of $x$\n\t\t- $(−1,7),(0,2),(1,1),(2,−4),(3,−7),(4,−2)$\n\t\t- Thus the code word is $(7,2,1,−4,−7,−2)$","x":1120,"y":1295,"width":585,"height":520},
		{"id":"bac3aa82e0048128","type":"text","text":"Algorithm 5 HQC.KEM.Encapsulate(pk = (h, s))\n- Provided the public key $(h, s)$\n1) $m \\leftarrow \\mathbb{F}_2^k$\n2) $salt \\leftarrow \\mathbb{F^{128}_2}$\n3) $\\theta := \\textit{G}(m||pk||salt)$ \n4) $c := (u,v)=HQC.PKE.Encrypt(pk,m,\\theta)$\n5) $K := \\textit{K}(m,c)$\n6) $d := \\textit{H}(m)$\n7) $return (\\textit{K}, (c, d, salt))$","x":1780,"y":-140,"width":585,"height":820},
		{"id":"3bc825e8fd561ed6","type":"text","text":"Algorithm 5 HQC.KEM.Decapsulate(sk = (x, y), c, d, salt)\n- Provided the secret key and the encapsulated message\n1) $m' := HQC.PKE.Decrypt(sk, c)$\n2) $\\theta' := \\textit{G}(m'||pk||salt)$\n3) $c' := (u', v') = HQC.PKE.Encrypt(pk, m', \\theta')$\n4) $d' := \\textit{H}(m')$\n5) $K' := \\textit{K}(m', c)$\n6) if $c \\neq c$ or $d \\neq d'$ then\n\t   $return (K', 0)$\n\telse\n\t\t$return (K', 1)$\n\tend if ","x":2440,"y":-140,"width":585,"height":820},
		{"id":"b0a341eeadaca61d","type":"text","text":"Polynomial Multiplication","x":198,"y":2031,"width":250,"height":50},
		{"id":"6efc1d08fdb187fc","type":"text","text":"Modular Inverse for Ints using FCT GCD [10]\n- Similar implementation to paper\n- Could not be used immediately as shift module was embedded with other modules\n- https://caslab.io/publications/deshpande2021modular.pdf","x":323,"y":2140,"width":382,"height":340},
		{"id":"74e275742c9ef059","type":"text","text":"Shift module from AMD [12]\n- Register based approach \n- Not scalable when length of input is as large as the n value for the HQC param\n- ++ Paper uses dual port BRAM and barrel rotation \n\t- Barrel rotation and BRAM widths are used as perf param\n- https://docs.amd.com/v/u/en-US/xapp195","x":-59,"y":2140,"width":382,"height":340},
		{"id":"3089e083b33fdb31","type":"text","text":"Accelerating HQC with Additive FFT\n- Processors\n\t- x86-64 with AVX2 and Galois Field New Instructions\n\t- ARM NEON on Apple M1\n\t- ARM NEON on Cortex-A72\n- https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/014\n- [2026-01-09]\n---\n- Implements an additive FFT with the Chinese Remainder Theorem \n\t- Accelerates polynomial acceleration\n\t- Cites challenge with polynomial degrees fractionally greater than powers of two have inefficient FFT implementations\n\t\t- Reduces redundant computations\n\t- Notes particular interest for FFT in terms of Encryption function\n\t\t- Function includes 2 polynomial multiplications that share a common input polynomial\n\t\t- Steps 2 and 4\n\t- Butterfly operation\n- Discusses two methods:\n\t- Kronecker Segmentation (KS) + CRT for short multiplication instructions (PMULL.P8)\n\t- Frobenius Additive FFT (FAFFT) for long multiplication instructions (PLCMULQDQ and PMULL.P64)\n- Further optimizations\n\t- Cached key transforms improves sampling process for Encrypt and Decrypt\n\t- Encrypt uses polynomial $r2$ for $u$ and $v$ polynomials - proposed method you can save a input transform \n- Fast Hadamard Transform for RM-RS encoding\n---\n- Implementation is slower in raw multiplication, but demonstrated gains by removing redundant computations\n- Concludes that crypto agility is important\n\t- In the sense that optimal choice of polynomial multiplication is dependent on target arch and ISA\n\t- Generalized differences between platforms with long mult instructions (PLCMULQDQ and PMULL.P64) and short mult instructions (PMULL.P8)\n- ","x":-97,"y":3080,"width":657,"height":1100},
		{"id":"2333c872d82cd6c4","type":"text","text":"Sparse Vector Reconstruction from Distance Spectrum using Soft Information\n\n- https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/2221\n- [2025-12-12]","x":1060,"y":3320,"width":657,"height":224},
		{"id":"87b53b3ba5b06503","type":"text","text":"Acceleration/impmenetations","x":95,"y":2980,"width":273,"height":50},
		{"id":"97c8fdb56b7e0b3c","type":"text","text":"Reed–Muller Encoding Leakage Enables Single-Trace Message Recovery in HQC\n\n- https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/071.pdf\n- [2026-01-16]","x":1060,"y":3080,"width":657,"height":224},
		{"id":"574fc0ab5fd2c3ba","type":"text","text":"Attacks","x":1264,"y":2980,"width":250,"height":50},
		{"id":"5c657565468852ae","type":"text","text":"You Only Decapsulate Once: Ciphertext-Independent Single-Trace Passive Side-Channel Attacks on HQC\n\n- Demonstrates side channel attacks and power side channel attacks\n\t- Can be mitigated if process is real time though...\n\n- https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/2162\n- [2025-12-01]","x":1060,"y":3560,"width":657,"height":224},
		{"id":"14e136ecd728b7f1","type":"text","text":"A Sparse Polynomial Multiplier for HQC Integrating Parallelism and Power-Based Side-Channel Countermeasures\n- FPGA\n\t- Xilinx Atrix-7 FPGA\n- https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/2003\n- [2025-10-30]\n---\n- Implements shift-add multipliers with dummy insertions and parallelization \n- Notes that code-based schemes require more robust side-channel-aware hardware countermeasures \n\t- Citing a paper demonstrating the power side-channel key recovery attack on a BIKE FPGA implementation \n\t- https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10838487\n- Shift-add multipliers were avoided in the official implementation, but were used by hardware implementations for efficiency\n\t- Underlying principle of shift-add multipliers is such that partial products of dense polynomial are shifted according to indices of sparse operand and accumulated via XOR\n\t- However, shift-add multipliers could be subject to correlation power analysis via the Hamming Weight and Hamming Distance models\n---\n- ","x":-97,"y":4200,"width":657,"height":1100}
	],
